    code: { lang: "python", title: "sqli_demo.py — vulnerable login, parameterized fix, regression test (complete, Python 3.10+, stdlib sqlite3)", code: "import sqlite3\n\ncon = sqlite3.connect(':memory:')\ncon.execute(\"CREATE TABLE users (name TEXT, pw_hash TEXT)\")\ncon.execute(\"INSERT INTO users VALUES ('nadia', 'scrypt$demo-hash')\")\n\ndef login_vulnerable(name):\n    # FLAW (deliberate, local demo only): input glued into SQL as syntax\n    q = \"SELECT name FROM users WHERE name = '\" + name + \"'\"\n    row = con.execute(q).fetchone()\n    return row[0] if row else None\n\ndef login_secure(name):\n    # FIX: the value is bound separately and can never become syntax\n    row = con.execute(\"SELECT name FROM users WHERE name = ?\", (name,)).fetchone()\n    return row[0] if row else None\n\npayload = \"' OR '1'='1\"\nprint('vulnerable login with payload:', login_vulnerable(payload))\nprint('secure login with payload:   ', login_secure(payload))\nprint('secure login, real user:     ', login_secure('nadia'))\n# Regression test — run in CI; the payload must NEVER authenticate again\nassert login_secure(payload) is None, 'REGRESSION: injection works again!'\nassert login_secure('nadia') == 'nadia', 'REGRESSION: real login broke!'\nprint('regression tests passed')", output: "vulnerable login with payload: nadia\nsecure login with payload:    None\nsecure login, real user:      nadia\nregression tests passed", lines: ["The vulnerable function is four lines — injection needs no exotic tooling, just string glue.", "The payload changes the WHERE clause's LOGIC; no password was guessed or stolen.", "The secure version differs by one mechanism: ? placeholder + a parameter tuple.", "The asserts are the regression test: attack must fail, real login must keep working — both halves matter.", "Run it with: python3 sqli_demo.py — then try a UNION-shaped input against both functions."] },
