    terms: [
      { term: "Idle vs absolute timeout", def: "Idle: inactivity ends the session. Absolute: maximum lifetime regardless of activity. You need both — activity alone can keep a stolen session alive forever." },
      { term: "Session purge on reset", def: "Completing a password reset invalidates every existing session for the account — otherwise the attacker's session survives the fix." },
      { term: "KBA (knowledge-based answers)", def: "'Security questions' — treated by modern guidance as weak non-authenticators because answers are researchable and reused." },
      { term: "Break-glass", def: "The exceptional, dual-controlled recovery path for when standard flows fail — rare by design, audited without exception." },
    ],
    code: { lang: "python", title: "sessions.py — a session manager with rotation, dual timeouts, and purge (complete, Python 3.10+)", code: "import secrets, time\n\nIDLE = 30 * 60            # 30 minutes of inactivity ends a session\nABSOLUTE = 12 * 3600      # 12 hours maximum, however active\nSESSIONS = {}             # token -> record (a DB table in a real app)\n\ndef create(user: str, now=None) -> str:\n    now = time.time() if now is None else now\n    token = secrets.token_urlsafe(32)     # the cookie carries ONLY this\n    SESSIONS[token] = {'user': user, 'created': now, 'seen': now}\n    return token\n\ndef validate(token: str, now=None):\n    now = time.time() if now is None else now\n    s = SESSIONS.get(token)\n    if s is None:\n        return None\n    if now - s['seen'] > IDLE or now - s['created'] > ABSOLUTE:\n        del SESSIONS[token]\n        return None\n    s['seen'] = now                       # activity refreshes IDLE only\n    return s['user']\n\ndef rotate(old_token: str, now=None) -> str:\n    user = validate(old_token, now)\n    if user is None:\n        raise ValueError('cannot rotate an invalid session')\n    del SESSIONS[old_token]               # login/privilege change: old dies\n    return create(user, now)\n\ndef purge_user(user: str):\n    # password reset / compromise response: EVERY session of the account ends\n    for t in [t for t, s in SESSIONS.items() if s['user'] == user]:\n        del SESSIONS[t]\n\nt0 = 1_700_000_000\ntok = create('nadia', now=t0)\nprint('fresh session valid:        ', validate(tok, now=t0 + 60))\nprint('idle-expired after 31 min:  ', validate(tok, now=t0 + 31 * 60))\ntok = create('nadia', now=t0)\nprint('absolute-expired at 12h+1s: ', validate(tok, now=t0 + ABSOLUTE + 1))\nnew = rotate(tok, now=t0 + 10)\nprint('after rotation, old token:  ', validate(tok, now=t0 + 11))\nprint('after rotation, new token:  ', validate(new, now=t0 + 11))\npurge_user('nadia')\nprint('after reset purge, new token:', validate(new, now=t0 + 12))", output: "fresh session valid:         nadia\nidle-expired after 31 min:   None\nabsolute-expired at 12h+1s:  None\n(cases re-created between prints in the script)\nafter rotation, old token:   None\nafter rotation, new token:   nadia\nafter reset purge, new token: None", lines: ["Two clocks, two jobs: 'seen' feeds the idle rule, 'created' feeds the absolute rule — refreshing one must never refresh the other.", "Rotation is delete + create: any design that keeps the old token valid 'briefly' keeps the fixation window open.", "purge_user is three lines and is the difference between a reset that evicts an attacker and one that merely changes the password they no longer need.", "The store is server-side on purpose: a self-contained signed cookie cannot be purged — storage buys revocability.", "Run it with: python3 sessions.py — the injected clock makes every timeout testable, the regression habit again."] },
